# A changelog sentence, in the commit that first wrote the changelog. The default `generic-api-key` rule
# read the prose `key, ` as an assignment and took the identifier after it as the value. That identifier
# is `application-secret-v1` — the name of a key, never key material: it is the public
# `ConfiguredSecretKeyRings::LEGACY_KEY_ID`, written into the `key_id` field of every envelope it sealed
# and refused by `assertIdentifier()` when an operator tries to reuse it, so it is already published in
# the database rows it labels and there is nothing in it to leak. The sentence now reads `key identified
# as` at the tip, which the rule does not match, so the shape cannot come back; this entry covers only
# the wording frozen in the commit that introduced it, which gitleaks still reads. Scoped to one commit,
# one file, one rule and one line — a real credential added to CHANGELOG.md still fails. The entry
# becomes wrong only if `application-secret-v1` ever names key material instead of naming a key.
# The fingerprint names the commit as it exists on `master`. A pull request that is rebased or squashed
# on merge rewrites its commits, so a fingerprint captured from a branch stops matching the moment the
# branch lands. Take the fingerprint from the merged history, not from the branch, or the scan fails on
# `master` after a green pull request.

